FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:941 — `truncate` documents "at most `width` terminal cells" but for `width < 3` computes `budget = width.saturating_sub(3) = 0`, breaks immediately, then appends `"..."`, returning 3 cells for a width of 0/1/2. The new unit test `edge_truncate_stays_within_small_widths_and_respects_wide_characters` hides this by asserting `<= width.max(3)` instead of `<= width`, so the contract is untested exactly where it is violated. — VERIFIED: traced the arithmetic path at tests/bench/mod.rs:945-957 (budget 0 then unconditional `short.push_str("...")`). — Fix: define the sub-3 behaviour (return `""` or clamp the ellipsis) and assert the real bound `UnicodeWidthStr::width(shortened) <= width`, not `width.max(3)`.

2. [STYLE] tests/bench_test.rs:1556 — `edge_baseline_parser_ignores_an_unshaped_unknown_row` claims an unrelated table cannot poison baseline parsing, but its input row has only two cells; any four-cell row whose first cell is not a known workload still returns an error at tests/bench/mod.rs:1227. The test therefore only exercises the pre-existing non-four-cell skip branch, not the "unshaped unrelated table" scenario its name and the REPORT advertise. — Fix: either narrow the test/doc claim to "a two-cell unrelated row is skipped", or make the parser ignore a four-cell row from an unrelated table and add the corresponding assertion.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
